    "title": {
     "en": "A short story"
    },
    "text": {
     "en": "Ravi touched a wire to a bulb circuit and it glowed. When he used a plastic scale, it did not.\n\"Some things let current pass,\" said the teacher."
    }
   },
   {
    "id": "explain",
    "type": "explanation",
    "provenance": "original",
    "keepEnglishTerms": [
     "conductor",
     "insulator",
     "fuse"
    ],
    "text": {
     "en": "Materials that let electric current pass are conductors, like copper, aluminium and iron. Materials that do not are insulators, like plastic, rubber, wood and glass.\nWires are made of copper covered with plastic. The metal conducts and the plastic keeps us safe.\nWe must never touch switches with wet hands or put fingers in sockets. A fuse or MCB breaks the circuit if too much current flows."
    }
   },
